{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (from GET /api/v1/releases/current, HTTP 200): the current stable release is SCOPe 2.08, reported with stable_label "2.08-stable" (db_name SCOPe, is_public true, is_comprehensive false, freeze_date 2021-07-28, release_date 2021-09-22, old_version null, api_url /api/v1/releases/2.08). The latest periodic update reported is version "2.08-2026-08-16", dated 2026-08-16.

Which identifier to cite: for a reproducible analysis, cite the stable public release selector, SCOPe 2.08 (stable label 2.08-stable), and use the release-scoped API paths under /api/v1/releases/2.08/... rather than the periodic label. Stable releases are frozen (freeze date 2021-07-28) and are the normal basis for reproducible work; a periodic update does not replace the stable release. If an analysis specifically depends on records that only exist in the periodic update, then the full periodic label (2.08-2026-08-16) and its date must be recorded in addition to the stable release, since "current" will change over time.

What the periodic label means: the label 2.08-2026-08-16 identifies a dated incremental update layered on top of the 2.08 stable release (the base version plus the update date). In SCOPe practice, periodic updates add newly classified PDB entries (typically automated assignments to existing families/superfamilies) between stable releases, without re-curating or renumbering the stable 2.08 hierarchy. However, the current-release response itself only reports labels and dates; it does not enumerate which records the 2026-08-16 update added or whether any classification changed, so those specifics cannot be verified from this endpoint alone. Note also that is_comprehensive is false, meaning the release does not claim to cover every PDB entry, and that citing bare "current" is not reproducible because the periodic version advances.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases/current","scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md"]}]}
